 ## Overview
 
-Naive Voxel Meshing is a surface extraction algorithm that converts a 3D voxel grid into a renderable mesh by iterating over every voxel and emitting geometry for all six faces of every solid voxel, regardless of adjacency [S2, S5, unofficial]. It solves the problem of translating discrete voxel data into vertex/index buffers without performing face culling or vertex sharing [S1, S5, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Face Culling Voxel Meshing](2268-face-culling-voxel-meshing.md), which checks neighbors to omit internal faces, Naive Voxel Meshing treats each voxel as an independent cube, resulting in a significantly higher polygon count and draw call overhead [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Naive Voxel Meshing is a surface extraction algorithm that converts a 3D voxel grid into a renderable mesh by iterating over every voxel and emitting geometry for all six faces of every solid voxel, regardless of adjacency [S2, S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of translating discrete voxel data into vertex/index buffers without performing face culling or vertex sharing [S1, S5, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Face Culling Voxel Meshing](2268-face-culling-voxel-meshing.md), which checks neighbors to omit internal faces, Naive Voxel Meshing treats each voxel as an independent cube, resulting in a significantly higher polygon count and draw call overhead [S1, S2, unofficial]. Some sources describe checking neighbors for face culling [S3, S5, unofficial], but the standard definition and performance metrics confirm it emits all six faces without adjacency checks [S2, S6, unofficial].
 
